    verification guardPoolDispatchesLongestFirstRetroGate : Test { :>> id = "8ace157a-3209-45e9-b263-e8566afc2b7a"; :>> title = "Sprint 657 retro gate (autonomous, D0049)"; :>> createdAt = "2026-09-10"; :>> createdBy = "claudeOpus5"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::analyze; :>> procedureText = "Autonomous retro (D0049). AVOIDABLE-ISSUE SCAN: one Issue recorded this sprint, issue458, and it was avoidable: a timing comparison stated from two windows without the A/A case, written into a code comment before the like-for-like run - D0388's class (a number stated before its check was probed against a known case), a third time after issue455 and sprint 655's retro finding; recorded for the priority lens (D0311) and given a control that is an instrument's refusal (--ab refuses non-interleaved sets, probed both ways), with its limit stated - nothing reads a number in a comment and knows its host state. Finding 2, a recurrence, not an Issue: textpatch insert_before anchored on a #[test] line split the run_all test from its doc comment exactly as in sprint 655 - caught by reading the diff, fixed by anchoring on the doc comment's first line; two occurrences of a slip whose fix is anchor choice, and a linter for 'a doc comment followed by a blank line then #[test]' would be the control if it recurs again - noted, not built, at two. Finding 3, not avoidable: the simulation's independent-duration assumption is the kind of thing only the measurement can falsify, and the DoD's PROBE-FIRST/DO-ONLY-IF shape is what made the wrong DO branch cheap to reverse. Finding 4, hygiene: 13 stale kernel JVMs were alive on a host where timing was being read; the sweep (kill_stale_kernels.py) ran mid-sprint, after they had already coloured two windows - a timing sprint starts with the sweep, which the probe's docstring now says."; }
